            <ArticleTitle>[Static disorders or venous pathology of the foot?].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>249-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">Three types of disorder observed in the foot may have either a mechanical or a vascular aetiology. They are: A) Painful or tired legs, caused by: eversion, inversion or arch collapse of one or both feet.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">corrective or compensatory soles depending on the joint mobility and the integrity of the skin. B) Metatarsalgia due to static disorders, incorrect shoes or vascular disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">orthopaedic soles, appropriate shoes. C) Burning pain in the toes exacerbated by heat, relieved by cold, due to claw or lateral deformity of the toes or compression of the forefoot and toes (shoes, stockings).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">appropriate shoes, protective or corrective orthoplasties.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
